.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1246 AM PDT Thu Jul 30 2026
(Friday through Wednesday)

No significant changes were made for the long term forecast as
overall forecast ideas remain valid from previous forecast. For
the highlights - Building high pressure will continue the inland
warmup. Saturday looks to be the warmest with interior Central
Coast making a run at 110. Temperatures moderate Sunday into
Monday as the ridge flattens. Interior locations will still see
widespread Moderate HeatRisk with a few isolated pockets of Major.
Shallow compressed marine layer will keep the coast from seeing
the real heat, but temperatures still warm a few degrees.
Temperatures rebound again next week with another push of warm
850mb air.

Now for what we`re watching closely. For a few days now the ECMWF
EFI has been showing really strong winds over the coastal waters
Saturday and especially Sunday. The project N-S pressure gradient
will be in the 7 to 9 mb range (99th percentile), which is very
strong. Hi- res guidance is showing Storm Force gusts over 60 mph
developing north of Point Reyes.

As noted by the day shift yesterday, there are a few bursts of
offshore flow. A more notable one is Sunday night/Monday morning
across the N Bay into SF Bay. Obvious fire weather impacts with
warming and drying, but temperatures at the coast have a high bust
potential if this pans out for Monday.

Finally, fire weather and beaches. See sections below for more
details. If you`re recreating in the heat this weekend be the "one
less spark one less fire". Additionally, if you`re seeking relief
from inland heat by heading to the beach please never turn your
back to the ocean. We still have hazardous conditions.

