.BEACHES...
Issued at 225 PM PDT Wed Jul 29 2026

Long period southerly to southwesterly swell of 15-17 seconds
will continue to impact our coastline over the next several days,
bringing hazardous beach conditions to the Central Coast and the
San Francisco Bay Area. Sneaker waves and strong rips currents
will be the primary conditions, with breaking waves up to 10 feet
along some south and southwest facing beaches and break points,
such as Santa Cruz, Pacifica, and parts of Stinson or Ocean Beach.
This can be particularly surprising when wave heights increase
suddenly after a period of seemingly tranquil wave action. In
fact, sneaker waves can have 20+ minutes in between the larger
sets (breaking waves) with significant "flat/calm looking" times
between them. Due to the long period, sneaker waves are powerful
and can catch beachgoers by surprise, resulting in people and pets
being swept into the cold, turbulent ocean. Rip currents are
strong enough to pull even the strongest swimmers away from shore.
Check marine conditions before you head out! Swim near a
lifeguard, if possible. Maintain a safe distance from the water,
and stay away from jetties, piers, beachside rocks, and other
waterside infrastructure. Never turn your back to the ocean and if
you have small children or pets at the beach, please keep an eye
on them. Hazardous beach conditions are anticipated to persist
through Saturday afternoon.

